      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "CI Checks Skill Execution Flow\n├── main() function entry point \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:25\n│   ├── skill := ci_checks.New() \u003c-- 1b\n│   └── skill.Run(ctx, req) \u003c-- 1c\n├── Skill.Run() method \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:56\n│   ├── checkRepoStatus(ctx) call \u003c-- 1e\n│   └── skill.Response{} return \u003c-- 1d\n└── checkRepoStatus() implementation \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:78\n    ├── GitHub API client setup \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:82\n    ├── CI status validation \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:88\n    └── Error handling \u0026 logging \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:72\n        └── fmt.Errorf() wrapper \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:72",
      "traceGuide": "# Motivation\n\nThe CI Checks skill solves the **critical problem** of automatically validating repository status and CI pipeline health. When developers need to know if their code changes have passed all checks, they typically need to manually check multiple systems. This skill automates that process by querying GitHub's API to retrieve combined status checks for specific commits, providing a centralized way to monitor CI health across different services and workflows.\n\n# Details\n\nThe skill follows a **standard foxctl pattern** [1a]. It begins in `main()` where the skill is instantiated via `ci_checks.New()` [1b] and executed through `skill.Run(ctx, req)` [1c]. The core logic lives in the `Run()` method [1d], which orchestrates the CI validation process.\n\nThe actual work happens in `checkRepoStatus()` [1e], where the skill:\n- Creates a GitHub API client to authenticate with GitHub\n- Retrieves the combined status for a specific commit SHA\n- Processes the results to determine overall CI health\n- Returns a structured response with status information\n\nError handling is built throughout the flow, with proper logging and graceful failure modes [1d-1e]. The skill integrates with foxctl's broader skill framework, making it discoverable and executable within the agent ecosystem."
    },
