      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Skill Framework Integration\n├── Import skill framework \u003c-- 3a\n├── Skill Structure Definition \u003c-- 3b\n├── Skill Constructor \u003c-- 3c\n│   └── Initialize skill instance \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:51\n└── Main Execution Flow\n    ├── skill.Run() method entry \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:56\n    │   └── Execute business logic \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:68\n    └── Response Formation \u003c-- 3d\n        └── Return formatted result \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:75",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe foxctl system needs a **standardized way** to execute automated tasks like CI checks and PR comment processing. Without a common skill framework, each task would have inconsistent interfaces, error handling, and deployment patterns. The skill framework solves this by providing a **uniform structure** that all skills follow, making them easy to discover, execute, and manage within the larger agent ecosystem.\n\n## Details\n\nSkills in foxctl follow a **consistent lifecycle** [3a]. Each skill imports the core skill framework, defines its structure [3b], and implements a constructor [3c]. The main entry point is always the `Run()` method [3c] which receives a context and request, executes the business logic, and returns a formatted response [3d]. This pattern allows the agent runtime to **dynamically load** and execute any skill without knowing its internal implementation details. The framework handles common concerns like error propagation and response formatting, letting skill developers focus on their **core business logic** rather than boilerplate infrastructure code."
    },
    {
      "id": "4",
      "title": "GitHub API Integration Layer",
