          "lineContent": "status, _, err := client.Repositories.GetCombinedStatus(ctx, owner, repo, sha)",
          "title": "CI Status Check",
          "description": "Retrieves combined CI status for a commit"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "GitHub API Integration Layer\n├── getGitHubClient() \u003c-- 4a\n│   └── Client Authentication Setup \u003c-- skills/ci_prcomments/main.go:82\n├── PR Operations Branch\n│   ├── PullRequests.Get() \u003c-- 4b\n│   │   └── Retrieves PR metadata \u003c-- skills/ci_prcomments/main.go:92\n│   └── Issues.ListComments() \u003c-- 4c\n│       └── Fetches PR comments \u003c-- skills/ci_prcomments/main.go:98\n└── CI Status Branch\n    └── Repositories.GetCombinedStatus() \u003c-- 4d\n        └── Gets commit CI status \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:88",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nThe CI checks and PR comment extraction skills solve the **critical need** for automated repository validation and feedback processing in development workflows. Without these tools, teams would manually need to check CI status and parse through PR comments to understand code review feedback, slowing down the development cycle significantly.\n\n## Details\n\nThe GitHub API integration layer provides **centralized access** to GitHub's functionality through a dedicated client [4a]. This client handles authentication and serves as the gateway for two main operation branches:\n\n**PR Operations Branch** focuses on pull request interactions. It retrieves PR metadata [4b] to understand the context and changes, then fetches all associated comments [4c] to gather code review feedback and discussions.\n\n**CI Status Branch** monitors the health of code changes by retrieving combined CI status for specific commits [4d], providing insight into whether tests, builds, and other automated checks have passed.\n\nThe integration follows a **clean separation** pattern where GitHub API calls are abstracted through the client, allowing both CI checks and PR comment skills to operate independently while sharing the same underlying infrastructure."
    },
    {
      "id": "5",
      "title": "Error Handling and Response Processing",
