          "description": "Exits with error code on failure"
        }
      ],
      "traceTextDiagram": "Error Handling \u0026 Response Processing\n├── main() function entry point \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:25\n│   ├── skill.Run(ctx, req) \u003c-- 5a\n│   │   ├── Error occurs during execution\n│   │   │   └── fmt.Errorf() wrap \u003c-- 5c\n│   │   └── Return error to caller\n│   ├── Error handling block \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:36\n│   │   ├── log.Printf() error log \u003c-- 5b\n│   │   └── os.Exit(1) failure exit \u003c-- 5d\n│   └── Successful execution path\n└── skill.Response{} formation \u003c-- skills/ci_checks/main.go:75\n    └── Return structured response",
      "traceGuide": "## Motivation\n\nWhen running automated CI checks and PR comment processing, **failures must be handled gracefully**. The system needs to provide clear error information to users while maintaining stability. Without proper error handling, failed operations would leave users confused about what went wrong and could cause the entire skill execution to crash unexpectedly.\n\n## Details\n\nThe error handling pattern follows a **consistent three-step approach**:\n\n1. **Error Detection** - The skill.Run() method [5a] catches errors during execution\n2. **Error Reporting** - Uses log.Printf() [5b] to output descriptive error messages for debugging\n3. **Graceful Exit** - Calls os.Exit(1) [5d] to terminate with proper error code\n\nFor internal errors, the system uses **error wrapping** with fmt.Errorf() [5c] to preserve context while adding meaningful descriptions. This creates a clear chain of responsibility from the low-level failure up to the user-facing error message.\n\nThe main() function [25] serves as the top-level orchestrator, ensuring that any failure in the skill execution pipeline is caught and handled consistently across both CI checks and PR comment processing skills."
    }
  ],
